          Muscle loss with age is not inevitable, but it is the default. From midlife onward, most people lose muscle and strength gradually, and by the seventies the loss can start to threaten balance, mobility and independence. Two things slow it more reliably than anything else: regular strength training and enough protein, eaten in large enough portions at each meal for older muscles to respond. The trouble is that appetite often shrinks at exactly the age protein needs rise. The best protein powder for seniors makes that gap easy to close: 20 to 30 g of high-quality protein, simple to open, mix or pour, and gentle on digestion. Here are seven picks, the targets expert groups recommend, and when to check with your doctor first.
