        <div id="sarcopenia" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Muscle Gets Harder to Keep After 60
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sarcopenia is the progressive loss of muscle strength, mass and function that comes with age. The European Working Group on Sarcopenia in Older People, whose 2019 definition is widely used, treats low muscle strength as the primary warning sign, confirmed by low muscle quantity or quality. It matters because it is linked with falls, fractures, slower recovery from illness and loss of independence, and because it often goes unnoticed until someone struggles to get out of a chair or climb stairs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Several things drive it at once. Physical activity tends to fall with age, hormones shift, illness and hospital stays cause sudden bouts of muscle loss that are hard to fully regain, and appetite often shrinks. On top of that, older muscle responds less vigorously to the protein you eat, a phenomenon researchers call anabolic resistance. A meal that would fully switch on muscle building in a 25-year-old may only partly do so at 70.
          </p>

          <p>
            The evidence that protein matters here is reasonably consistent. In the Health, Aging and Body Composition study, which followed more than 2,000 adults in their seventies for three years, people in the highest fifth of protein intake lost about 40 percent less lean mass than those in the lowest fifth. That study was observational, so it cannot prove cause and effect, but it fits with feeding studies showing that older adults need more protein per meal to get a robust muscle response.
          </p>

          <p>
            A protein powder is not a treatment for sarcopenia, and it does nothing without some form of strength training. What it does well is close the gap between what many older adults eat and what expert groups recommend, especially at breakfast and on low-appetite days. For more on how protein drives muscle building, see our explainer on <a href="/muscle-protein-synthesis-supplements/">muscle protein synthesis</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Older muscles need bigger protein doses and a reason to grow.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Anabolic resistance means a small serving of protein does less after 60. Aim for about 25 to 30 g at each main meal, and pair it with strength training two or more days a week. A shake makes the first part easy.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked: Dose, Leucine and Ease of Use
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run lab tests, taste panels or clinical trials for this guide. The picks are based on label analysis, published third-party certifications and the research on protein needs in older adults, the same method described on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. Comments on taste, texture and mixing reflect what buyers widely report, not results of our own.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first criterion was dose. Because expert groups point to roughly 25 to 30 g of protein per meal for older adults, we favored products that reach that range in a single serving, or come close enough that a splash of milk closes the gap. The second was protein quality, which in practice means leucine, the amino acid most closely tied to switching on muscle building. Dairy proteins such as whey and milk protein are naturally rich in it, so most picks are dairy-based, with one plant option for those who need it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third criterion was ease of use, which matters more for this group than for any other. Arthritis, weaker grip, poorer eyesight and smaller appetites all change what counts as a good product. We gave credit for ready-to-drink cartons and bottles, for powders that buyers say dissolve with a spoon rather than needing a blender, and for unflavored options that can be stirred into everyday food. Very large tubs with stiff lids lose points in practice, even if they look good on price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we looked at sugar, testing and digestion. Most picks have one or two grams of sugar per serving, which suits the many older adults managing blood glucose. We noted NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice status where brands claim it, flagged lactose where it may cause trouble, and noted products whose added vitamins and minerals matter for people on certain medications.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1.0-1.2</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">g/kg/day PROT-AGE and ESPEN suggest for healthy older adults</p>
              </div>
