        <div id="how-much" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Much Protein Older Adults Need: PROT-AGE and ESPEN
          </h2>

          <p>
            The US recommended dietary allowance for protein is 0.8 g per kilogram of body weight per day for all adults, regardless of age. Many researchers argue that figure is too low for older people, because it was set to prevent deficiency rather than to preserve muscle, and because it does not account for anabolic resistance or the extra demands of illness.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two influential expert groups addressed this directly. In 2013, the PROT-AGE Study Group, an international panel convened with support from the European Union Geriatric Medicine Society, published recommendations in the Journal of the American Medical Directors Association. It advised that adults over 65 eat on average at least 1.0 to 1.2 g of protein per kilogram per day, at least 1.2 g per kilogram for those who exercise or are otherwise active, and 1.2 to 1.5 g per kilogram for most older adults with acute or chronic illness. In 2014, an expert group convened by ESPEN, the European Society for Clinical Nutrition and Metabolism, reached a similar conclusion: at least 1.0 to 1.2 g per kilogram for healthy older adults, 1.2 to 1.5 g per kilogram for those who are malnourished or at risk because of illness, and protein combined with daily physical activity or exercise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both groups made an important exception. PROT-AGE explicitly excluded people with severe kidney disease, an eGFR below 30, who are not on dialysis, noting they may need to restrict protein. That is covered in more detail below, but it is the main reason to talk to a doctor before raising protein intake.
          </p>

          <p>
            To translate those figures, a 70 kg, or roughly 154 lb, older adult would aim for about 70 to 84 g a day on the 1.0 to 1.2 g range, compared with 56 g on the RDA. Surveys suggest many older adults fall short of the higher figure, particularly those who live alone, have small appetites or have recently been ill. Our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> and guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> do the arithmetic for your own weight.
          </p>

