          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best protein powder for seniors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most older adults, a whey-based product that delivers about 25 to 30 g of protein per serving, such as Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard mixed with milk, or a ready-to-drink shake like Ensure Max Protein if mixing is a chore. Whey is rich in leucine, which older muscles need more of. Choose the format you will actually use daily.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much protein does a 70-year-old need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Expert groups including PROT-AGE and ESPEN suggest at least 1.0 to 1.2 g per kilogram of body weight per day for healthy older adults, and 1.2 to 1.5 g per kilogram during illness. For a 70 kg person that is about 70 to 84 g daily. People with severe kidney disease need individual advice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is protein powder good for older adults?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be, as a convenient way to close the gap between what many older adults eat and what experts recommend, especially at breakfast or with a small appetite. It works best alongside strength training and a food-first diet. Check with your doctor first if you have kidney disease or take several medications.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Ensure or a protein powder better for seniors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both work. Ready-to-drink shakes like Ensure Max Protein need no mixing and suit small appetites, poor grip or busy caregivers. Powder costs much less per gram of protein and can be stirred into food. Many older adults use powder at home and keep a few shakes for convenience.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can protein powder help with sarcopenia?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Protein alone does little, but combined with resistance training it can help maintain and build muscle in older adults. Meta-analyses show modest extra gains in lean mass and strength when protein is added to a training program. Sarcopenia should be assessed by a doctor, who can rule out other causes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is whey protein safe for seniors with kidney problems?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on how severe the kidney disease is. People with an eGFR below 30 who are not on dialysis may need to limit protein, while people on dialysis often need more. Ask your doctor or nephrologist for an individual protein target before adding any supplement if you have kidney disease.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When should seniors take protein powder?</h3>
